  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:smooth-face-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If each cell survivor mass is positive and every endpoint has a unique separated projection, minimum, and cut branch, then \\(\\mathrm{CI}_n\\) is first-order equivalent to the ordinary joint Gaussian delta-method interval based on the two active quotient gradients.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "\\(\\mathrm{CI}_n\\)",
      "\\(g^L_{n,r}\\)",
      "\\(g^U_{n,r}\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:oracle-face-equivalence",
      "ass:oracle-separation"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the required regular-case reduction and guards against paying for nonexistent ties.",
    "gap": "It recovers the familiar smooth endpoint limit as a corollary rather than imposing smoothness on the main class.",
    "consumer": "It lets standard software and influence-function calculations validate the new implementation on well-separated empirical designs.",
    "proof_tex": "Under the stated premise and \\(\\texttt{ass:oracle-separation}\\), there are unique compatible active regions \\(r_L\\) and \\(r_U\\) for the two endpoint numerators, with the common projection and survivor-minimum choices fixed. The separation margin and\n\\(\\lVert\\widehat w_n-w_n\\rVert=O_{P_n}(n^{-1/2})\\) imply that the segment from \\(w_n\\) to \\(\\widehat w_n\\) stays in those regions with probability tending to one. Positivity of each cell survivor mass ensures that the displayed quotient formulas are defined there; the aggregate denominator is already bounded below in the model class.\n\nLet \\(g_n^L=g^L_{n,r_L}\\) and \\(g_n^U=g^U_{n,r_U}\\). On the stable regions, the ordinary quotient rule and the first-stage Taylor expansion give\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\theta_{L,n}-\\theta_{L,n})\n =g_n^{L\\prime}n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\psi_{w,n}(O_{ni})+o_{P_n}(1),\n\\]\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\theta_{U,n}-\\widehat\\theta_{U,n})\n =g_n^{U\\prime}n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\psi_{w,n}(O_{ni})+o_{P_n}(1).\n\\]\nBy \\(\\texttt{thm:oracle-face-equivalence}\\), the retained family equals these active choices with probability tending to one, and its critical value differs by \\(o_{P_n}(1)\\) from\n\\[\n q_{1-\\alpha}\\!\\left(\n \\max\\{g_n^{L\\prime}G_n^*,g_n^{U\\prime}G_n^*\\}\n \\mid O_{n1},\\ldots,O_{nn}\\right).\n\\]\nThis is exactly the ordinary joint one-sided Gaussian delta-method critical value for the two signed endpoint errors. The two expansions and critical-value equivalence show that the corresponding lower and upper confidence limits differ from those of the ordinary joint Gaussian interval by \\(o_{P_n}(n^{-1/2})\\). Clipping at the known support boundaries is common to both constructions and preserves this first-order equivalence.",
